# ThreadSanitizer suppressions.
#
# Keep this file SHORT and every entry justified. A suppression is a claim that
# a reported race is not real; an unjustified one hides the bugs TSan exists to
# find. Prefer fixing the code every time it is our code.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# trantor::Logger lazy statics
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reported as: data race in trantor::Logger::~Logger() (Logger.cc:490) on the
# std::function returned by Logger::outputFunc_() / flushFunc_().
#
# Both are function-local statics ("magic statics"), whose initialisation IS
# thread-safe per [stmt.dcl]/4 — GCC implements it with __cxa_guard_acquire /
# __cxa_guard_release, which TSan normally understands.
#
# It is reported anyway because vcpkg builds libtrantor.a WITHOUT TSan
# instrumentation. The inline accessor is emitted in both trantor's objects and
# ours; the linker keeps one copy, and when the surviving copy comes from the
# uninstrumented archive TSan observes the loads and stores but not the guard
# synchronisation around them. That is a false positive.
#
# Evidence it is not our thread pool: only the one test that logs at ERROR from
# two workers simultaneously ever reported it. The tests that hammer the queue
# with eight concurrent producers never did.
#
# The real fix is building every dependency with -fsanitize=thread. That is a
# full from-source rebuild of the dependency graph, so it belongs in a dedicated
# CI job rather than in every developer's loop. Revisit if trantor is ever built
# instrumented, and delete this entry then.
race:trantor::Logger::~Logger
race:trantor/utils/Logger.h

